# Slot‐Die and Roll‐to‐Roll Processed Single Junction Organic Photovoltaic Cells with the Highest Efficiency

## abstract
The record efficiency of the state‐of‐the‐art polymer solar cells (PSCs) is rapidly increasing, due to the discovery of high‐performance photoactive donor and acceptor materials. However, strong questions remain as to whether such high‐efficiency PSCs can be produced by scalable processes. This paper reports a high power conversion efficiency (PCE) of 13.5% achieved with single‐junction ternary PSCs based on PTB7‐Th, PC71BM, and COi8DFIC fabricated by slot‐die coating, which shows the highest PCE ever reported in PSCs fabricated by a scalable process. To understand the origin of the high performance of the slot‐die coated device, slot‐die coated photoactive films and devices are systematically investigated. These results indicate that the good performance of the slot‐die PSCs can be due to a favorable molecule‐structure and film‐morphology change by introducing 1,8‐diiodooctane and heat treatment, which can lead to improved charge transport with reduced carrier recombination. The optimized condition is then used for the fabrication of large‐area modules and also for roll‐to‐roll fabrication. The slot‐die coated module with 30 cm2 active‐area and roll‐to‐roll produced flexible PSC has shown 8.6% and 9.6%, respectively. These efficiencies are the highest in each category and demonstrate the strong potential of the slot‐die coated ternary system for commercial applications.

The record efficiency of the state-of-the-art polymer solar cells (PSCs) is rapidly increasing, due to the discovery of high-performance photoactive donor and acceptor materials. However, strong questions remain as to whether such high-efficiency PSCs can be produced by scalable processes. This paper reports a high power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $13.5\%$ achieved with single-junction ternary PSCs based on PTB7-Th, $\mathsf{P C}_{71}\mathsf{B M}$ , and COi8DFIC fabricated by slot-die coating, which shows the highest PCE ever reported in PSCs fabricated by a scalable process. To understand the origin of the high performance of the slot-die coated device, slot-die coated photoactive films and devices are systematically investigated. These results indicate that the good performance of the slot-die PSCs can be due to a favorable moleculestructure and film-morphology change by introducing 1,8-diiodooctane and heat treatment, which can lead to improved charge transport with reduced carrier recombination. The optimized condition is then used for the fabrication of large-area modules and also for roll-to-roll fabrication. The slot-die coated module with $30c m^{2}$ active-area and roll-to-roll produced flexible PSC has shown $8.6\%$ and $9.6\%$ , respectively. These efficiencies are the highest in each category and demonstrate the strong potential of the slot-die coated ternary system for commercial applications.
Over the past few decades, solution-processed bulk-heterojunction (BHJ) polymer solar cells (PSCs) have continued to demonstrate their potential as a high-efficiency and cost-effective next energy source to provide light-weight, flexibility, and roll-to-roll processed mass production.[1–8] In particular, the
PSC-efficiencies have increased with intensive effort on novel materials synthesis, film-morphology engineering, and interface control, and the state-of-the-art PSCs have eventually produced a high power conversion efficiency (PCE) of over $15\%$ .[9–21] For example, Zou et  al. reported a newly synthesized nonfullerene acceptor (NFA), Y6, having an electron-deficient-core-based fused ring with a benzothiadiazole unit, and the resulting single-junction PSCs based on PM6:Y6 BHJ showed an excellent efficiency of $15.7\%$ and a certified PCE of $14.9\%$ .[20] In addition, Hou and co-workers recently reported a series of copolymers, and in particular, the copolymer T1 (poly[(2,6-(4,8-bis(5-(2-ethylhexyl)- 4-fluorothiophen-2-yl)-benzo[1,2-b:4,5-b′] dithiophene))-alt-(5,5- $[1^{\prime},3^{\prime}$ -di-2-thienyl$5^{\prime},7^{\prime}$ -bis(2-ethylhexyl)benzo[ $1^{\prime},2^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{c}{:}4^{\prime},5^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{c}^{\prime}]$ dithiophene-4,8-dione)] $(\mathrm{PBDB{\cdot}T F})~=~0.8\$ and $\mathrm{PTO}2~=~0.2;$ ) produced the bestefficiency of $15.1\%$ and certified PCE of $14.6\%$ in PSCs using 3,9-bis(2-methylene-((3-(1,1-dicyanomethylene)-6,7- difluoro)-indanone))-5,5,11,11-tetrakis(4- hexylphenyl)-dithieno $[2,3\mathrm{-d};2^{\prime},3^{\prime}\mathrm{-d}^{\prime}]$ -s-indaceno[1,2-b:5,6-b′] dithiophene (IT-4F) acceptor.[21]
One of the representative routes to further enhance PSCefficiency is broadening the absorption bandwidth of the active film for better sunlight absorption.[21–23] To this end, the ternary
PSC strategies that include one donor/two acceptors or two donors/one acceptor could be effective methods to provide higher PCEs than binary systems, by careful third-component selection.[22–24] In particular, polymer:fullerene:nonfullerene based ternary-blend PSCs, combining the merits of fullerene to provide high electron mobility and nonfullerene acceptors to provide strong near-infrared (NIR) or visible absorption, achieved over $10\%$ PSC-efficiencies.[25,26] Recently, Ding and coworkers reported a novel low-band gap nonfullerene acceptor, COi8DFIC, with strong NIR absorption, and the ternary PSCs based on PTB7-Th, $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ , and COi8DFIC showed an excellent PCE of $14.08\%$ , higher than that of PTB7-Th:COi8DFIC (1:1) binary PSCs $(12.16\%)$ .[27,28] More recently, through further optimization, the PTB7-Th:COi8DFIC: $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ ternary combination produced a record PCE of $14.62\%$ .[29] However, the outstanding device-efficiencies have mostly been obtained by using a laboratory-only process, i.e., spin coating. The method is not scalable and also not roll-to-roll-compatible. Therefore, the development of scalable deposition techniques is urgently needed for the manufacture of low-cost, high-efficiency, and roll-to-roll produced PSCs.[30,31]
Despite these challenges, only a few groups have shown success in achieving high performance PSCs by scalable methods.[31–35] For example, Yan and co-workers showed that blade-coated PSCs with PffBT4T-2OD: $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ based BHJ can produce a high PCE of up to $9.9\%$ .[35] Hou and coworkers also presented the excellent PCE of $9.5\%$ in doctor blade-coated PSCs based on PTB7-Th and 3,9-bis(2-methylene(3-(1,1-dicyanomethylene)-indanone))-5,5,11,11-tetrakis(4- hexylphenyl)-dithieno $^{2,3-\mathrm{d}:2^{\prime},3^{\prime}-\mathrm{d}^{\prime}]}$ -s-indaceno $[1,2\mathrm{-}\mathsf{b};5,6\mathrm{-}\mathsf{b}^{\prime}]$ dithiophene) (ITIC) NFA.[31] In particular, $12.88\%$ PCE was achieved in doctor blade-coated PBDB-TF:IT-4F PSCs through controlling the surface morphology of the $z\mathrm{nO}$ interlayer;[36] and more recently, Hou and co-workers demonstrated a top efficiency of $13.1\%$ from the blade-coated PSCs based on T1:IT4F.[21] Although the abovementioned studies showed promising results, the blade coating method could have limited practicality in manufacturing, especially for roll-to-roll production, due to the method having difficulty in controlling film-thickness and fabricating stripe patterns; and in fact, there has been no report on the roll-to-roll fabrication of blade-coated PSCs. Meanwhile, slot-die coating can be considered as the most suitable method for the roll-to-roll production of PSCs. The method is classified as a premetered method, so that the thickness of films can be easily and precisely controlled, simply by controlling the solution flow into the head. In addition, the method uses $100\%$ material in theory, and is capable of producing stripe patterns, which is essential to produce series-connected modules.[37–39] However, the slot-die method has not been well studied and the efficiency of slot-die based devices has still remained low, compared to the high PSCs in the recent reported spin-coating based PSCs.[39,40] Furthermore, the PCEs of roll-to-roll slot-die PSCs have been only within ${\approx}(5-7)\%^{[39-41]}$ and the $7.32\%$ PCE has been known as the top record efficiency of the roll-to-roll processed PSC.[42] More importantly, it should also be recognized that the differences in drying kinetics and fluid dynamics in different deposition methods can induce different results during the solution-to-solid evolution processes. Therefore, an already optimized process for a champion device-efficiency is not guaranteed to produce high performance in other scalable deposition methods.[43–47] Moreover, large fractions of highperformance materials are not suitable for roll-to-roll slot-die coating.[48,49] These facts motivate us to put more effort into the realization of high-performance PSCs by a scalable method.
Here, we present a high cell-efficiency of $13.5\%$ realized with single-junction PSCs based on PTB7-Th, $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ , and COi8DFIC fabricated by slot-die coating, which produces the highest power conversion efficiency in the literature among the printing-based PSCs. To reveal the mechanism of the high PSC-efficiency obtained with slot-die coating, the photovoltaic parameters, quantum efficiencies, molecular structures, filmmorphology, and charge recombination/transport behaviors were investigated. To demonstrate the scalability of the developed method, the condition was used to fabricate seriesconnected modules and achieved an impressive PCE of $8.6\%$ . More importantly, the slot-die deposition condition developed by the batch process was translated to roll-to-roll processing equipment and resulted in roll-to-roll processed flexible PSCs with a PCE of $9.57\%$ , which is the highest PCE to date. The study not only demonstrates high-efficiency PSCs via slot-die coating strategies but also provides significant insights into their practical applications in roll-to-roll based PSC-production.
Figure  1a shows the molecular structures of the one electron donor (PTB7-Th) and two electron acceptors $(\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ and COi8DFIC) used in this study. Figure  1b shows the PSCstructure with an inverted configuration (indium tin oxide $\mathrm{(ITO)/ZnO/PTB7.Th.PC_{71}B M:C O i8D F I C/M o O_{3}/A g)}$ , which constitutes a more compatible configuration for fully printed or vacuum-free PSC-production. An optimized ternary blend of PTB7-Th: $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ :COi8DFIC (1:0.45:1.05, w:w:w) was utilized,[28,29] and the ternary combination was first processed as a novel photoactive layer by the slot-die technique shown in Figure  1c. Here, all active layers were slot-die-coated with a 3D-printer-based slot-die coating equipment (3DP slot-die), which was recently developed as an effective mid-step tool in the lab-to-fab translation to test the feasibility of lab-scale printed devices for roll-to-roll based PSC-production.[50–52] The 3DP slotdie platform can provide accurate $X,$ Y, and $Z$ positions and solution-feed controls through PC-digital commands, and the coating system can be readily repeated via simple PC-commands, thus allowing for the printed PSCs to be highly reproducible, and for the roll-to-roll coating process to be mimicked.[39,50] Figure  1c and Figure S1 in the Supporting Information show a schematic of the slot-die coating process and photographic images of the 3D-printer based slot-die coater, respectively.
To find the slot-die processing optimum conditions and directly evaluate the effect of the newly slot-die coated PTB7- T $\mathrm{h}{:}\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ :COi8DFIC on the photovoltaic characteristics, various PSCs were fabricated and compared. For the PSCoptimization, a systematic and fine adjustment of the ternary concentration, additive concentration, and heat-treatment, which are well recognized as the most important and foremost conditions for PSC-performances,[9–14] was conducted and sequentially optimized, as shown in Figure $2\mathrm{a-c}$ plotted using ${\approx}15$ devices. Throughout the blend-concentration optimization, the ternarysolution concentration of $13.5~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ was adopted, which yields average PSC-efficiency of $10.82\%$ , as shown in Figure 2a and in Table S1 in the Supporting Information. With the introduction of 1,8-diiodooctane (DIO) into the ternary active solutions, the slot-die coated PSCs showed more improved cell-efficiencies, with average PCE of $11.85\%$ , as shown in Figure 2b and in Table S2 in the Supporting Information. The best conditions were then used to optimize the heat-treatment, and as shown in Figure 2c and in Table S3 in the Supporting Information, the post-thermal annealing produced the highest PSC-performance, with an average PCE of $12.91\%$ . For better comparison, three representative printed PSCs were fabricated under each optimal condition and showed a great influence on the PSC efficiencies. Figure  2d shows the photovoltaic performances of the slot-die coated devices prepared under the blendsolution concentration of $13.5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ (pristine), $13.5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ ternary-concentration, and $0.5~\mathrm{vol}\%$ DIO treatment (DIO), and $13.5~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}$ ternary, $0.5\%$ DIO, and post-thermal annealing optimum conditions (DIO/Annealing). The representative $J{-}V$ data and Table  1 show that by controlling the ternary concentration only, the pristine slot-die coated PSC revealed a PCE of $11.23\%$ , short-circuit current density $(J_{\mathrm{sc}})$ of $24.13\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ fill factor (FF) of $65.05\%$ , and open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ of $0.72{\mathrm{~V}}.$ Although the obtained PCE was quite high, the overall PSC-performances did not approach those of the spin-coated PTB7-Th: $\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ :COi8DFIC devices, which showed PCEs of over $14\%$ .[28,29] However, as shown in Figure 2d, and in Table 1, with the use of the DIO and thermal annealing treatments (DIO and DIO/annealing), all PSC-efficiencies were improved and the improved PCEs resulted from the enhancement in $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and FF, rather than $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ . The DIO-based PSC showed a PCE of $12.32\%$ , $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $25.58\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , FF of $67.87\%$ , and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $0.71\mathrm{V};$ and introducing thermal annealing further raised the PCE to $13.5\%$ , the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ to $27.12~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , the FF to $69.84\%$ , and the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ to 0.71 V. This result closely approached the values of the spincoated ternary counterpart PSCs, and more encouragingly, the PCE is the highest value ever reported in scalable coating-based PSCs. These photovoltaic device results and trends were further supported by studies of the external quantum efficiency (EQE) and internal quantum efficiency (IQE) shown in Figure  2e,f, respectively. The pristine device exhibited an EQE peak of $\approx80\%$ , and the DIO and DIO/Annealing based PSCs showed more improved EQE peaks, reaching ${\approx}(83$ and $86\%$ , respectively. The calculated $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ from the EQE data of the pristine, DIO, and DIO/Annealing devices are (22.94, 24.90, and 25.76) mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , respectively, and these $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ -values are quite close to those from the $J{-}V$ curves within ${\approx}5\%$ mismatch. In particular, compared to the pristine, the DIO device showed the EQE enhancement of up to 1.10 times, and with the thermal annealing, the EQE was further improved up to 1.03 times for DIO/Annealing relative to DIO. These EQE-enhancement degrees are well matched with the IQE-enhancement (1.08 times for DIO relative to the pristine, and 1.04 times for DIO/Annealing relative to DIO). Here, considering that the EQE changes were mainly due to the
IQE changes and the IQE is tightly linked to charge transport/ collection,[14,53] it was believed that the EQE and $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ improvement in DIO and DIO/Annealing PSCs could be due to the charge transport/collection enhancement induced with the DIO and annealing treatments for slot-die active-layer deposition. These observations support that both the additive and thermal annealing treatments can significantly impact the device performance of the slot-die-printed ternary PSCs. More importantly, these results show that with careful and independent optimization of the slot-die active-layer deposition conditions, high-performance printing-based polymer solar cells can be achieved with scalable and roll-to-roll manufacturable slot-die techniques, with maintaining the quite high efficiencies mainly achieved under lab-scale spin-coating.
In order to investigate the molecular ordering structures of slot-die coated photoactive polymer-films, grazing incidence wide angle X-ray scattering (GIWAXS) analysis of PTB7- Th: $\mathrm{\Delta}[\mathrm{PC}_{71}\mathrm{BM}$ :COi8DFIC blended films was performed on three representative conditions, i.e., the pristine, with DIO, and with DIO/Annealing. Figure S2 in the Supporting Information shows the in-plane and out-of-plane direction profiles, while Table S4 in the Supporting Information summarizes the obtained crystalline packing parameters. Figure  3a–c shows that the GIWAXS images reveal that all the blended films have face-on and edgeon orientations. The GIWAXS image of the pristine blended film exhibits weak X-ray reflection around $1.63\ \mathring\mathrm{\mathrm{A}}^{-1}$ (out-of-plane direction, $\left.q_{z}\right)$ , corresponding to the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking(010) of PTB7-Th with face-on orientation. The blended film with DIO shows the sharper reflection peaks of the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking $(010)$ around $1.65\textup{\AA}^{-1}$ $(q_{\mathrm{z}})$ , and lamella(100) packing around $0.28\mathrm{\textrm{\AA}}^{-1}$ (in-plane direction, $q_{\mathrm{xy}})$ . Furthermore, the GIWAXS image of the blended film with DIO/Annealing reveals that the DIO additive and thermal annealing treatment clearly improves the face-on orientation. The blended film with DIO/Annealing clearly shows pronounced face-on orientation with the intensified $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking(010) intensity in the out-of-plane direction $(q_{z}\approx1.67\mathring\mathrm{A}^{-1})$ , and lamella(100) packing around the in-plane direction $(q_{\mathrm{xy}}\approx0.28\textup{\AA}^{-1})$ . Moreover, the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking distances of the blended films are calculated to be (3.86, 3.81, and 3.77) $\mathrm{\AA}$ for the Pristine, DIO, and $\mathsf{D I O}/$ Annealing, respectively. These results clearly indicate that slot-die coated films with additive and thermal annealing treatments decrease the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking distance of face-on orientation, leading to highly improved intermolecular interaction and charge transport in the blended film,[54–63] and thus resulting in high $J_{\mathrm{sc}},$ FF, and PCE. For better understanding of the enhanced PSCperformances with slot-die coating, the nanoscale-morphology of the printed ternary-blend films was studied using atomic force microscopy (AFM), as presented in Figure  3d–f. As shown in Figure  3d, the pristine blend films slot-die processed with no DIO and thermal annealing showed a quite smooth surface and a fine phase-separation, with $0.93\ \mathrm{nm}$ root-mean-square (RMS) roughness, highly similar to the previously reported spin-coating based ternary films,[28] which indicates that the slot-die coated ternary blend film can also produce superior nanoscale phase-separation and good miscibility of the one donor and two acceptors in their ternary mixed films for efficient exiton-dissociation.[64,65] On the other hand, compared to the pristine films, the DIO and DIO/Annealing films presented somewhat different morphology with relatively rougher surface with more distinct phase-separation known to provide better charge-transport,[65,66] which supports that controlling the slot-die deposition condition enables tuning of the nanoscale-morphology of the photoactive blend films. In addition, compared with the DIO-based films with $1.34~\mathrm{nm}$ RMS, the printed films processed with both DIO and thermal annealing conditions resulted in a rougher RMS surface $(1.46~\mathrm{nm})$ that possessed more distinct phase-separation and surface-corrugation for superior carrier-transport/collection and better FF and $J_{\mathrm{sc}},^{[39,66]}$ which could result from the crystallinity improvement in the solid films slot-die processed under the DIO/Annealing conditions.[65–67] Notably, these favorable morphology and molecule-structure changes achieved in the DIO and DIO/Annealing-based films could be responsible for the greater PSC-characteristics shown in the slot-die printed devices.
As discussed earlier, we aimed to develop a process that could be scaled up. Therefore, we first tried to use the optimized condition to fabricate large-area series-connected modules. Since the 3DP platform provides fully automated $x{-}y{-}z$ position control, we simply programmed the coating length to be extended, and the coating to be repeated with a regular cell interval, while keeping other deposition parameters consistent with those of small cells. Since the process was developed with the consideration of upscaling at the start, the upscaling was straightforward, and the module showed an excellent PCE of $8.6\%$ , as shown in Figure  5a. Here, the PCE losses in modules could be mainly attributed to an increase in the series resistance across the cell with increasing cell area.[50,74] However, the module efficiency of $8.6\%$ is a quite impressive value, and more importantly, we believe that this PCE is the highest PCE of solution-processed PSC module in the peer-reviewed literature. We found a higher PCE only in a proceeding report that describes a $9.5\%$ module, without disclosing details of the materials and processing parameters.[75]
The batch process was then translated to a roll-to-roll system to demonstrate the potential of high-performance rollto-roll printed flexible PSCs. The inset of Figure 5b shows the flexible PSCs and the roll-to-roll deposition system used for this demonstration. A key difference between the batch and roll-to-roll processes is the time constraint in the roll-to-roll process. Solutions are deposited on a continuously moving substrate. Therefore, time-consuming processes, such as solvent soaking technique, cannot be used in the roll-to-roll process. This could be one of the hurdles in the commercialization of PSCs. Another constraint is the temperature limitation of flexible substrates. Commercially available flexible transparent conducting electrodes (TCEs) are typically made of polyethylene terephthalate (PET), which can tolerate up to (140–150) $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ range. In this specific work, ZnO layer was prepared via a sol–gel route, and annealed at high temperature $(200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ , to achieve maximum performance. Therefore, we used a commercially available $z\mathrm{nO}$ nanoparticle solution that was developed for flexible PSCs, and an already developed deposition condition, as described in the experimental section. On the $z\mathrm{nO}$ coated TCE-PET, we tried to produce the active layer that was as same as possible to the optimized film processed by the 3DP slot-die coater. Coating speed was matched to the batch process, and the same-design slot-die head was used, as shown in Figure S1 in the Supporting Information. The thickness of the roll-to-roll produced film was monitored in situ using an optical method that we have used in previous works[76,77] and matched to that of the optimized device by controlling the solution feed rate to the slot-die head. The slot die is known to be a premetered method, and the film thickness is linearly related to the feed rate. Therefore, we could easily achieve the desired thickness of the film. The film was then cut into small pieces and translated to the nitrogen-filled glove box for the thermal annealing and metal deposition. After metal deposition, the flexible PSCs were tested under nitrogen without encapsulation, and Figure 5b shows the $J{-}V$ curve of the best device. The champion device showed a PCE of $9.57\%$ with $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of 0.68, $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $23.5\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , and FF of $59.80\%$ . Here, the efficiency gap between batch and roll-to-roll slot-die processed devices could be due to the use of electrodes having a higher sheet resistance, undesirable physical contact during processing, or leakage current of the fragile ITO materials on flexible substrates, which can induce worse series and shunt resistances.[39,40,42] More importantly, the performance is still impressive, and the loss in the transition from the batch on glass to continuous roll-to-roll process on flexible substrate was minimal. A $J{-}V$ curve of a successively slot-die coated device fabricated on glass is also shown in Figure 5b for comparison. The device on glass showed up to $12.4\%$ with $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $0.70{\mathrm{V}},$ $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $25.53\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , and FF of $67.43\%$ . PCE variations of glassbased and flexible devices are also compared in Figure 5c and Table S5 in the Supporting Information, summarized with ten individual devices. We found similar PCE deviation from the batch and roll-to-roll processed ones, showing excellent device uniformity and reproducibility. For flexible roll-to-roll processed PSCs, it was unavoidable to get failed devices, due to the manual handling of the delicate flexible film after rollto-roll coating. Therefore, a small fraction of failed devices, i.e., devices with significantly lower $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ were not included in the figure. We believe that when devices are fully roll-to-roll processed by multihead commercial equipment, such failure is not an issue. Regardless, the performance of the champion device is undoubtedly the best among the roll-to-roll processed PSCs. Therefore, we performed a literature survey on the progress of the record efficiencies of PSCs fabricated by scalable processes, i.e., nonspin coating. In fact, blade coating has been dominating record PCEs of the nonspin coating category, and the scalable process is more likely blade coating. As this work reports slot-die coating, the method is categorized separately from other scalable methods.[34,37,39,40,42,78–82] We found the record PCEs of slot-die coated PSCs have been surprisingly low, as shown in Figure 5d. Table S6 in the Supporting Information summarizes the sources of each data point. It is clear that this work shows a leap of progress on slot-die coated PSCs and paves the way for the commercialization of PSCs.
In conclusion, we have demonstrated high-efficiency slot-die coated single-junction ternary PSCs with a $13.5\%$ PCE, which is the highest PCE among scalable coating-based PSCs. This remarkable device-efficiency was achieved with careful and systematic optimization of the slot-die active-layer-deposition conditions. The influence of the slot-die active-layer conditions was investigated by means of EQE, GIWAXS, AFM, internal device resistances, light-intensity dependent $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ results, and photo-CELIV measurements. The mechanism of the improved PSC-performances was believed to be mainly due to the more preferred morphological and molecular-structure changes, the more suppressed carrier recombination, and the better carrier transporting ability. In addition, for the fully slot-die processed PSCs, the solution-processible $\mathrm{{znO}}$ interlayer was also deposited by the slot-die process. The successive slot-die coated PSCs showed very competitive PCE of $12.4\%$ from small cells and $8.6\%$ from a large-area module with $30\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ of active area and five cells in series connection. More notably, the slot-die coating condition was readily translated into the roll-to-roll process, which produced flexible PSCs with up to $9.57\%$ of PCE, which is the highest among the devices fabricated by roll-to-roll process. This work clearly shows a leap of progress on slot-die coated PSCs and paves the way for the commercialization of PSCs.
## Experimental Section
Device Fabrication (Slot-Die Coating and Roll-to-Roll Process): The ITOpatterned glass substrates $(70\Omega\ s q^{-1}$ , Advanced Materials Glass Co., Ltd (AMG)) were cleaned sequentially by ultrasonic chamber in acetone, deionized water, and 2-propanol for $10~\mathsf{m i n}$ , respectively, and then exposed to $\mathsf{U V}/\mathsf{O}_{3}$ for $30\mathrm{\min}$ . For the preparation of $Z n O$ layer, zinc acetate $(0.1~\mathrm{~m~}$ , Sigma-Aldrich) dissolved in ethanol and ethanolamine was spin coated at 4000  rpm for $60\ s$ onto the $\mathsf{U V}/\mathsf{O}_{3}$ -treated ITO substrates, followed by annealing at $200^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for 10 min. For the active layer, PTB7-Th: $\mathsf{P C}_{71}$ BM:COi8DFIC (1:0.45:1.05, w/w/w, 1-material) blended solutions in chlorobenzene solvent $(7m L)$ were prepared with varying ternary blended concentration and DIO (Tokyo Chemical Industry Co., Ltd (TCI)) additive-concentration, which were then stirred at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for more than $12\mathrm{~h~}$ . The active solution was printed onto $Z n O$ layers using a 3D printer (clone-K200) based slot-die coater. The gap height between target-substrate and the slot-die head was set at about $100~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ , using a thickness gauge (Swan N.65M). The printing speed and solution volume were $3\ m m\ s^{-1}$ and $\mathsf{1~\ensuremath{\upmu L}~c m^{-2}}$ , respectively, and the slot-die head with $50~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ shim and $13m m$ thin meniscus guide was used. The resulting films were prepared with pre- or postheat treatment at $80~^{\circ}C$ for $10\min$ and all printing processes were carried out in air. For the fabrication of scalable fully printed devices, the ZnO layer was also slot-die-processed under identical procedures as those of the printed active layer. Finally, ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{3}$ $0_{3}(3\mathsf{n m})/\mathsf{A g}(80\mathsf{n m})$ electrodes were deposited by a thermal evaporator under vacuum at a pressure of $10^{-6}$ Torr through a metal mask, to provide a photoactive area of $0.1\ \mathsf{c m}^{2}$ . For the module device, the active layer solution was prepared the same as for the small scale device. For the patterned photoactive coating process, a slot-die coater the same as for the $z_{\mathsf{n O}}$ coating procedure with a $13\:\mathsf{m m}$ five-stripe pattern is used. The optimum slot-die coating condition of active layer used followed the optimum condition of the small scale device. All printing experiments and sample preparation were carried out in air. For the fabrication of top electrodes, the samples were transferred to a thermal evaporator, after the films were annealed at $80~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $10\min$ . Module-device fabrication was completed by thermal evaporation of the ${\mathsf{M o O}}_{3}/{\mathsf{A g}}$ metal top electrode $(3/750\mathsf{n m}$ thickness) in a vacuum with a pressure of $10^{-6}$ Torr. The device area of the PSC module was $30c m^{2}$ of active area with five cells. Roll-to-roll coated devices were fabricated using a customized commercial machine (Mini-Labo, Yasui Seiki) on a custom-patterned flexible TCE $\left(8\ \Omega\ \mathsf{s q}^{-1}\right.$ , OPV8 substrate manufactured by Solutia and patterned by Meko Print) film, without cleaning. ZnO $(2.8~\mathrm{w}/\mathrm{v}\%$ in acetone, InfinityPV) roll was deposited at $\mathsf{l}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ speed using 200R reverse gravure coating roll at 10  rpm. The film was dried by hot air $(720\ ^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ for ${\approx}10~\mathsf{s}$ and annealed on a curved hot plate ( ${\mathsf{l o o}}\ {\mathsf{o}}_{\mathsf{C}}$ for ${\approx}30~\mathsf{s}$ and ${\mathsf{120}}\ {\mathsf{^{o}C}}$ for ${\approx}10~\mathsf{s}$ ). The film was rewound and the optimized photoactive solution was deposited at $0.2\mathrm{~m~}\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ and solution feed rate of $25~{\upmu\updownarrow}~\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ (the calculated wet film is $9.6~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ based on $13\ m m$ coating width) to produce the optimized film thickness of (110–120) nm. The film was cut into small pieces, and transferred to a nitrogen-filled glove box for metal deposition. The films were annealed at $80~^{\circ}C$ for $10\mathrm{\min}$ , and $\mathsf{M o O}_{3}\ (3\ \mathsf{n m})/\mathsf{A g}\ (7\mathsf{50}\ \mathsf{n m})$ was deposited under similar conditions as described above. The device area of the flexible devices was $\mathsf{14}\mathsf{m m}^{2}$ .
Device Characterization: The $J{-}V$ characteristics of glass-based cells were measured using Keithley 2400 source-meter and Class AAA solar simulator (Oriel Instruments) at a light intensity of AM $\mathsf{1.5~C}$ $\mathsf{l o o m w c m}^{-2}$ . The J–V characteristics of the flexible PSCs were measured under inert condition in a glove box. A 150 W Xenon lamp (Newport) coupled with an AM $\mathsf{1.5~C}$ solar spectrum filter was used as the light source. Light was illuminated through a quartz window of the glove box, and the intensity was calibrated and monitored using a secondary reference cell (Hamamatsu S1133, with KG-5 filter, $2.8\ m m\times2.4$ mm of photosensitive area), which was calibrated by a certified reference cell (PV Measurements, certified by; National Renewable Energy Laboratory (NREL)), by using the Oriel AAA solar simulator fitted with a 1000 W Xe lamp. The external and internal quantum efficiency of PSCs was measured using IQE-200 series (Oriel Instruments). GIWAXS studies were carried out at the Pohang Accelerator Laboratory with the PLS-II 9A U-SAXS beamline, where the beam with $0.1775\ n m$ was incident on the samples under an angle of $0.13^{\circ}$ , and GIWAXS patterns were estimated with a charge-coupled device (CCD) detector (model Rayonix 2D SX165, PI-SCX: 4300). The film morphology, such as the topography and phase image, was investigated using AFM (XE7, Park system). The optical properties of the film were measured using UV–vis spectrometer (UV–vis–NIR Spectrophotometer LAMBDA 750, PerkinElmer). The light intensity was measured at various points by controlling the neutral density filter wheel (Newport). Photo-CELIV were performed using multifunctional organic semiconductor parameter test system (T4000, McScience) including Tektronix oscilloscope (DPO-2014B) at ${\mathsf{100~M H z}}$ . The carrier mobility $(\mu)$ , which is applied in a $525\ \mathsf{n m}$ laser pulse with width 100 fs and no delay time, was calculated using the equation below
$$
\mu=\frac{2d^{2}}{3A t_{\mathrm{max}}^{2}\left[1+0.36\frac{\Delta j}{j(0)}\right]}i f\Delta j\leq j(0)
$$
where $d$ is the film thickness, A is the voltage rise speed, $t_{\mathsf{m a x}}$ is the maximum extraction peak time, $\Delta j$ is the maximum current $(\Delta j=j\mathsf{m a x}-j(0))$ , and $j(0)$ is the displacement current.[71–73]
